# Windows App Alternatives

Switching to Linux doesn't mean giving up your favourite apps. Most of what you use daily has a native Linux version or a great alternative.

[[toc]]

## Browsers

No changes needed here — all major browsers are available on Linux.

| Windows | Linux Alternative | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Chrome | [Chrome](https://www.google.com/chrome/) | Native Linux version available |
| Firefox | [Firefox](https://www.mozilla.org/firefox/) | Pre-installed on most distros |
| Edge | [Edge](https://www.microsoft.com/edge) | Native Linux version available |
| Brave | [Brave](https://brave.com) | Native Linux version available |

## Office & Productivity

| Windows | Linux Alternative | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Microsoft Office | [LibreOffice](https://www.libreoffice.org) | Free, opens .docx/.xlsx/.pptx files |
| Microsoft Office | [OnlyOffice](https://www.onlyoffice.com) | Closer look and feel to MS Office |
| Notepad | [Kate](https://kate-editor.org) / [gedit](https://gedit-technology.github.io) | Feature-rich text editors |
| Notepad++ | [Kate](https://kate-editor.org) | Syntax highlighting, tabs |

::: tip Microsoft 365 in the browser
If you rely heavily on Word, Excel, or PowerPoint, you can use **Microsoft 365 online** at [office.com](https://www.office.com) — it works perfectly in any Linux browser, no installation needed.
:::

